Speculative Decoding Is Less Speculative Than You Think Guanning Zeng Andrea Zanette Affiliation: Carnegie Mellon University Abstract Speculative decoding accelerates language-model inference by preparing candidate continuations before the target model generates them. Its efficiency depends on how well limited drafting computation anticipates the path the target will actually follow. Anticipating this path involves two sources of uncertainty: the target model’s predictions and the randomness of sampling. Yet the former requires a target-model forward pass, while the latter can be fixed before drafting. Building on this observation, we introduce DICE (Draw-Informed Candidate Selection), which pre-samples and synchronizes verification randomness across drafting and verification. By combining these known draws with predictions from an existing drafter, DICE allocates computation toward continuations more likely to be used in the current sampling run. This principle guides node selection throughout draft-tree construction and extends to asynchronous prefetching, where DICE predicts which next-round drafts to prepare. Without additional drafter training or changes to the target sampling distribution, DICE achieves state-of-the-art decoding throughput across diverse settings, with speedups of up to 24.6% over strong baselines. 1 Introduction Speculative decoding (SD) is the art of anticipating the future. Before a large target model generates its next tokens, a lightweight drafter prepares plausible continuations, allowing the target to replace several sequential inference steps with a single parallel verification pass. But the drafter cannot afford to explore every possible continuation. Its efficiency therefore depends on allocating limited computation to the paths the target will actually follow. Anticipating the target’s continuation involves two sources of uncertainty: unknown target predictions and sampling randomness . The former arises because the drafter cannot fully predict the target model’s output distribution. The latter persists even with fixed target and draft distributions, as the sampling outcome can vary with the random draws. For example, in standard speculative sampling ( Leviathan et al., 2023 ; Chen et al., 2023 ) , a proposed token x x is accepted if u ​ q ​ ( x ) ≤ p ⁡ ( x ) , u\,q(x)\leq p(x),\addcontentsline{lla}{section}{
umberline q:acceptance} (1) where p ⁡ ( x ) p(x) and q ⁡ ( x ) q(x) are its target and draft probabilities, respectively, and u ∼ Uniform ⁡ ( 0 , 1 ) u\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1) is the verification draw. Much of the progress in speculative decoding has focused on the former, improving draft architectures ( Cai et al., 2024 ; Li et al., 2024b ; Chen et al., 2026 ) and training methods ( Zhou et al., 2024 ; Zhang et al., 2025 ; Li et al., 2025 ) to bring the draft distribution q q closer to the target p p . Yet the two sources of uncertainty differ in one crucial respect: obtaining the target’s predictions requires a costly forward pass, while the random draws used for verification can be generated before drafting begins. Could this very source of uncertainty help us decide what to draft? Figure 1 illustrates this with a fixed proposal x x and q ⁡ ( x ) = 0.5 q(x)=0.5 . When u = 0.1 u=0.1 , x x is accepted whenever p ⁡ ( x ) ≥ 0.05 p(x)\geq 0.05 ; when u = 0.9 u=0.9 , acceptance requires p ⁡ ( x ) ≥ 0.45 p(x)\geq 0.45 . The target probability is still unknown, but the acceptance threshold is already known! A small draw may favor looking further ahead through x x , while a large draw may favor preparing alternatives in case x x is rejected. Thus, the same draft prediction can lead to different allocations of speculative computation. Figure 1 : Known sampling randomness guides speculative computation. Both rows share the same proposal x x with q ⁡ ( x ) = 0.5 q(x)=0.5 , but differ in the verification draw u u . Dark and light purple indicate the values of p ⁡ ( x ) p(x) that would accept or reject x x , respectively. The dashed arrows illustrate how the same drafting budget can be allocated toward more lookahead through x x or more alternatives in case of rejection. The same idea also applies to Gumbel-max sampling, where the random perturbations used for target sampling can likewise be generated before drafting. Building on this observation, we introduce DICE (Draw-Informed Candidate Selection), named for the random draws that guide its drafting decisions. DICE combines these draws with draft-model predictions to guide speculative computation. In draft-tree construction, the resulting scores guide candidate generation, tree expansion, and final node selection. We further apply DICE to asynchronous prefetching, using known random draws to predict verification outcomes and prioritize next-round drafts. DICE works with existing draft models and preserves the exact target sampling distribution. We evaluate DICE across diverse target–drafter pairs, generation tasks, and inference settings. Under matched tree-node and prefetch-cache budgets, DICE improves acceptance length and cache hit rate, yielding decoding throughput gains of up to 24.6% and 13.7% in tree-based decoding and asynchronous prefetching, respectively. In optimized inference engines such as SGLang and vLLM, DICE achieves the highest decoding throughput among the evaluated methods, with gains across sampling temperatures, concurrency levels, and hardware configurations. Our contributions are summarized as follows: • We identify revealed sampling randomness as an underexplored source of information for allocating speculative computation. • We introduce DICE , a training-free, lossless framework that exploits this information throughout draft-tree construction and in asynchronous prefetching. • We demonstrate consistent speedups and state-of-the-art decoding throughput across diverse models, tasks, and inference settings. 2 Preliminaries Draft-Tree Verification. Tree-based speculative decoding organizes candidate continuations into a draft tree for parallel verification ( Miao et al., 2024 ; Chen et al., 2024 ; Wang et al., 2025 ) . Given a prefix h h , each node x x represents a continuation of length | x | |x| , and x ​ v xv denotes its child obtained by appending token v v . The tree contains at most N N non-root nodes. The target evaluates all candidate prefixes in a single forward pass. Verification then follows the target’s token choices from the root until the selected child is absent from the tree, accepting one token per visited non-root node and generating one additional token at the stopping node. We express target sampling using the Gumbel-max trick ( Maddison et al., 2014 ) : y x = arg ⁡ max v ​ { z x ​ ( v ) + T ​ g | x | + 1 ​ ( v ) } , 	extstyle y_{x}=\arg\max_{v}\{z_{x}(v)+Tg_{|x|+1}(v)\},\addcontentsline{lla}{section}{
umberline q:target-choice} (2) where z x z_{x} denotes the target logits after h ​ x hx , T > 0 T>0 is the sampling temperature, and g i g_{i} is a vector of independent standard Gumbel draws for the i i th position after h h . This yields the target sampling distribution softmax ⁡ ( z x / T ) \operatorname{softmax}(z_{x}/T) . Speculative Sampling and Asynchronous Prefetching. In standard speculative sampling ( Leviathan et al., 2023 ; Chen et al., 2023 ) , the drafter proposes a sequence x 1 : K x_{1:K} , which the target evaluates in a single forward pass and verifies token by token. A proposal x i x_{i} is accepted if u i ​ q i ​ ( x i ) ≤ p i ​ ( x i ) u_{i}q_{i}(x_{i})\leq p_{i}(x_{i}) , where p i p_{i} and q i q_{i} are the target and draft distributions at position i i , and u i ∼ Uniform ⁡ ( 0 , 1 ) u_{i}\sim\mathrm{Uniform}(0,1) is drawn independently of the proposal. At the first rejection, verification retains the accepted prefix and samples a correction token from the residual distribution proportional to [ p i − q i ] + [p_{i}-q_{i}]_{+} . If all proposals are accepted, a bonus token is sampled from the target distribution. We denote the verification outcome by ( k , v ) (k,v) , where k k is the number of accepted draft tokens and v v is the correction or bonus token. The next round starts from h x 1 : k v hx_{1:k}v . Speculative speculative decoding (SSD) ( Kumar et al., 2026 ) prepares drafts for possible next-round prefixes while the target verifies the current proposal. Each prefetched draft corresponds to a verification outcome ( k , v ) (k,v) , and the cache holds at most B B entries. If the actual outcome matches a ready cache entry, the next round can reuse the prefetched draft without waiting for another drafting pass. 3 Method In this section, we introduce DICE , a simple method for further accelerating speculative decoding. The key technical design is to draw the randomness used for verification before drafting and synchronize these draws between the drafting policy and the target verifier. The drafting policy combines them with predictions from an existing draft model to decide which continuations to prepare, while the verifier uses the same draws to determine the actual output. In this way, DICE improves the allocation of speculative computation without additional draft-model training or changes to the target sampling distribution. We instantiate DICE in two settings: (i) draft-tree construction (Section 3.1 ), where it guides node selection throughout tree construction, and (ii) asynchronous prefetching (Section 3.2 ), where it prioritizes next-round drafts for prefetching. Figure 2 : DICE uses pre-sampled randomness to guide draft-tree construction. Given the same prefix, draft model, and node budget ( N = 6 N=6 ), standard EAGLE constructs its tree using draft predictions alone, while DICE combines them with known Gumbel draws to guide node selection throughout tree construction. The score insets illustrate how the known perturbations change token preferences. The target uses its true logits and the same synchronized draws to produce the realized continuation shown on the right. Colored nodes indicate accepted draft tokens, while dashed nodes indicate the next target token absent from the tree. In this example, DICE accepts three draft tokens compared with one for EAGLE. 3.1 Seed-Aware Draft-Tree Construction At each node x x , the target’s next-token choice depends on its logits z x z_{x} and the Gumbel perturbation g | x | + 1 g_{|x|+1} (Eq. 2 ). As discussed earlier, obtaining z x z_{x} requires a target-model forward pass, whereas g | x | + 1 g_{|x|+1} can be sampled before drafting. We combine the draft logits d x d_{x} with the known perturbation to estimate the target’s next-token choice: r ^ x ​ ( v ) = softmax v ⁡ ( α ​ d x + T ​ g | x | + 1 σ ) , 	extstyle\widehat{r}_{x}(v)=\operatorname{softmax}_{v}\left( rac{\alpha d_{x}+Tg_{|x|+1}}{\sigma}ight),\addcontentsline{lla}{section}{
