rmed Candidate Selection), named for the random draws that guide its drafting decisions. DICE combines these draws with draft-model predictions to guide speculative computation. In draft-tree construction, the resulting scores guide candidate generation, tree expansion, and final node selection. We further apply DICE to asynchronous prefetching, using known random draws to predict verification outcomes and prioritize next-round drafts. DICE works with existing draft models and preserves the exact target sampling distribution. We evaluate DICE across diverse target–drafter pairs, generation tasks, and inference settings. Under matched tree-node and prefetch-cache budgets, DICE improves acceptance length and cache hit rate, yielding decoding throughput gains of up to 24.6% and 13.7% in tree-based decoding and asynchronous prefetching, respectively. In optimized inference engines such as SGLang and vLLM, DICE achieves the highest decoding throughput among the evaluated methods, with gains across sampling temperatures, concurrency levels, and hardware configurations. Our contributions are summarized as follows: • We identify revealed sampling randomness as an underexplored source of information for allocating speculative computation. • We introduce DICE , a training-free, lossless framework that exploits this information throughout draft-tree construction and in asynchronous prefetching. • We demonstrate consistent speedups and state-of-the-art decoding throughput across diverse models, tasks, and inference settings. 2 Preliminaries Draft-Tree Verification. Tree-based speculative decoding organizes candidate continuations into a draft tree for parallel verification ( Miao et al., 2024 ; Chen et al., 2024 ; Wang et al., 2025 ) . Given a prefix h h , each node x x represents a continuation of length | x | |x| , and x ​ v xv denotes its child obtained by appending token v v . The tree contains at most N N non-root nodes. The target evaluates all candidate prefixes in a sin