ween the drafting policy and the target verifier. The drafting policy combines them with predictions from an existing draft model to decide which continuations to prepare, while the verifier uses the same draws to determine the actual output. In this way, DICE improves the allocation of speculative computation without additional draft-model training or changes to the target sampling distribution. We instantiate DICE in two settings: (i) draft-tree construction (Section 3.1 ), where it guides node selection throughout tree construction, and (ii) asynchronous prefetching (Section 3.2 ), where it prioritizes next-round drafts for prefetching. Figure 2 : DICE uses pre-sampled randomness to guide draft-tree construction. Given the same prefix, draft model, and node budget ( N = 6 N=6 ), standard EAGLE constructs its tree using draft predictions alone, while DICE combines them with known Gumbel draws to guide node selection throughout tree construction. The score insets illustrate how the known perturbations change token preferences. The target uses its true logits and the same synchronized draws to produce the realized continuation shown on the right. Colored nodes indicate accepted draft tokens, while dashed nodes indicate the next target token absent from the tree. In this example, DICE accepts three draft tokens compared with one for EAGLE. 3.1 Seed-Aware Draft-Tree Construction At each node x x , the target’s next-token choice depends on its logits z x z_{x} and the Gumbel perturbation g | x | + 1 g_{|x|+1} (Eq. 2 ). As discussed earlier, obtaining z x z_{x} requires a target-model forward pass, whereas g | x | + 1 g_{|x|+1} can be sampled before drafting. We combine the draft logits d x d_{x} with the known perturbation to estimate the target’s next-token choice: r ^ x ​ ( v ) = softmax v ⁡ ( α ​ d x + T ​ g | x | + 1 σ ) , 	extstyle\widehat{r}_{x}(v)=\operatorname{softmax}_{v}\left( rac{\alpha d_{x}+Tg_{|x|+1}}{\sigma}ight),\addcontentsline{lla}{section}{
